  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 505,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 544,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 71507,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 292,
        "headingId": 544,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Testis and/or paratestis {11469344}</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-25T17:20:55"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 506,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 545,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 71508,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 292,
        "headingId": 545,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Metastases to the testis and paratestis usually occur in patients with a history of cancer {18769334}. In 10% of cases, the first manifestation is testicular enlargement, a palpable testicular mass, or hydrocoele {19624595}. Metastases occur mainly in adulthood, but uncommon cases may arise in children {15502808}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-25T17:20:55"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 507,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 546,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 71509,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 292,
        "headingId": 546,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Metastasis accounts for 2.4&ndash;3.6% of testicular tumours, and 8.1% of malignant tumours of the paratestis are metastatic. Most metastases are from the prostate (33%) or lung (20%), with other sources being skin melanoma (8%), appendix and colorectal malignancy (8%), kidney (6%), stomach, and pancreas {29548865; 25873879; 30305152; 28559825}. Haematopoietic tumours may involve the testis {9158685}. In children, sources include neuroblastoma, Wilms tumour, and rhabdomyosarcoma {11012739}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-25T17:20:55"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 508,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 547,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 71510,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 292,
        "headingId": 547,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-25T17:20:55"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 509,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 548,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 71511,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 292,
        "headingId": 548,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Metastasis may take several routes: retrograde lymphatic extension from para-aortic lymph nodes, spermatic vein extension, or arterial embolism {5343486}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-25T17:20:55"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 516,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 555,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 71512,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 292,
        "headingId": 555,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Metastatic tumours are most commonly a solitary nodule (62% of cases, with equal distribution between the left and right testes), or they are multiple nodules (17% of cases). In 21% of cases there is diffuse involvement of the organ {19624595}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-25T17:20:55"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 510,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 549,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 71513,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 292,
        "headingId": 549,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>A multitude of secondary tumour types can be seen. The most common growth patterns are interstitial (40% of cases), destructive (26%), and nodular (21%). Intratubular growth can be observed in lymphomas and in carcinomas from the prostate, kidney, and bladder. Infiltration of the rete testis can mimic a primary rete testis carcinoma. <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/attachment/36/292/22594\" target=\"_blank\">#22594</a>Table&nbsp;13.01 summarizes the secondary neoplasms of the testis and paratestis that pose diagnostic challenges and the primary tumours they mimic.</p>\r\n<p>Appropriate immunohistochemical studies are usually necessary for the identification of the origin of metastatic tumours {15502808}. Lineage-directed markers (e.g. PSA) are helpful but must be used with caution because some patterns overlap with those of primary tumours {19624595}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-25T17:20:55"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 511,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 550,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 71514,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 292,
        "headingId": 550,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>FNA cytology has a limited role in the diagnosis of metastases. Metastases can appear cytologically indistinguishable from primary tumours of the testis {3693570}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-25T17:20:55"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 512,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 551,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 71515,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 292,
        "headingId": 551,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-25T17:20:55"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 515,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 554,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 71516,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 292,
        "headingId": 554,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> a testicular or paratesticular mass with morphological features of another site of origin.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> known history of the primary malignancy; confirmatory immunohistochemistry.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-25T17:20:55"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 513,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 552,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 71517,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 292,
        "headingId": 552,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Metastases to the testis and paratestis are staged using the Union for International Cancer Control (UICC) system according to the primary tumour.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-25T17:20:55"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 514,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 553,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 71518,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 292,
        "headingId": 553,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Metastasis indicates advanced disease and a poor prognosis. The diagnosis helps determine the appropriate therapy {31885973}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-25T17:20:55"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]